steps.body:
Fourfold test – a dispute is non-arbitrable when:
1. It relates to actions **in rem** that do not pertain to subordinate rights in personam;
2. It affects **third-party rights**, has erga omnes effect, or requires centralised adjudication;
3. It relates to **inalienable sovereign and public interest functions** of the State;
4. It is **expressly or by necessary implication non-arbitrable** under a statute.
Held: landlord–tenant disputes under the Transfer of Property Act are arbitrable, but not those governed by rent control statutes; fraud is arbitrable unless it vitiates the arbitration agreement itself or involves serious criminal/public law aspects (also A. Ayyasamy, 2016).

The plaint shall be rejected:
(a) where it does not disclose a **cause of action**;
(b) relief **undervalued** and not corrected within time fixed;
(c) insufficient **stamp/court fee** and not made good;
(d) suit appears from the statement in the plaint to be **barred by any law**;
(e) not filed in **duplicate**;
(f) non-compliance with R9 (copies for service).
Only the plaint averments are seen, not the defence. Rejection is a decree, appealable; a fresh plaint may be filed on the same cause (O7 R13).

A person aggrieved by a decree or order (appealable but no appeal filed, or not appealable) may apply for review to the **same court** on:
1. **Discovery of new and important matter or evidence** which, after due diligence, was not within his knowledge or could not be produced;
2. **Mistake or error apparent on the face of the record**;
3. **Any other sufficient reason** (analogous to the above).
Review is not an appeal in disguise. Limitation: 30 days (Art 124).
